<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rifa'a al-Tahtawi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1801">1801</a>–<a href="/years/1873">1873</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Rifa'a at-Tahtawi, Rifa'a Rafi' al-Tahtawi, Rifaa al-Tahtawi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rifa'a al-Tahtawi was a 19th-century Egyptian writer, teacher and translator, an early figure of the Nahda, the Arab renaissance. A scholar of al-Azhar, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1826 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> to a student mission sent by Muhammad Ali and read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> there; his account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Takhlis al-ibriz fi talkhis Bariz, appeared in 1834. In 1835 he founded the School of Languages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, and he supervised the translation of more than 2,000 foreign works into Arabic. His guide to educating girls and boys alike was published in 1873.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rifaa-al-tahtawi.jpg" alt="Rifa'a al-Tahtawi" width="150" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tahtawi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
